<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Africa</h1><p class="st-meta">1146 CE – 1160 CE · capital Mahdia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Norman Kingdom of Sicily's string of coastal conquests in Ifriqiya, a short-lived Christian dominion over Muslim ports from Tripoli to Mahdia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily</a> exploited famine and the collapse of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zirid-emirate">Zirid emirate</a> to seize the North African coast. His admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-of-antioch">George of Antioch</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a> in 1146, and in 1148 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdia">Mahdia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sousse">Sousse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfax">Sfax</a> fell as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zirid-dynasty">Zirid</a> ruler al-Hasan fled. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabes">Gabès</a> and other towns accepted Sicilian overlordship, forming what Roger styled his realm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Normans ruled indirectly, as they did with the Muslims of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a> and governors stayed in place, Islamic law and worship continued, and the towns paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>. Garrisons held the ports, and the aim was control of grain and the sea lanes between Sicily and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> rather than settlement or conversion.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Roger's death in 1154 the towns revolted, starting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfax">Sfax</a> in 1156. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a> marched east, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdia">Mahdia</a> and took it in January 1160, ending Christian rule. The episode foreshadowed later European footholds on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> coast.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-africa.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Africa" width="320" height="367"><figcaption>Image: IvanBondarev · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royaume_de_Sicile_en_1154.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1146 CE – 1160 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mahdia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Roger II, William I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (subjects); Catholic Christianity (rulers)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, <a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Norman Africa, Norman Ifriqiya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1153&amp;focus=kingdom-of-africa" aria-label="Kingdom of Africa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4960 895 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Africa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5280 1151L5255 1145M5280 1151L5333 1064M5280 1151L4794 1282"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5255 1145h0M5333 1064h0M4794 1282h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5280 1151h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5280 1151h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mahdia. <a href="/atlas?year=1153&amp;focus=kingdom-of-africa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

